cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  A Latch-On-Resistant, False-Alarm-Gated Lead-Time Metric for Bearing
  Anomaly Detection - and What It Reveals About SCADA-Rate Logging
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Ansh
    family-names: Goyal
    affiliation: "Jaypee Institute of Information Technology, Noida, India"
    email: anshgoyal500@gmail.com
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"  # add your ORCID, then uncomment
repository-code: "https://github.com/Ansh-Goyal01/Scada-Leadtime-Benchmark"
abstract: >-
  A reproducible benchmark that makes lead time, gated by a leakage-free
  false-alarm rate, the primary metric for evaluating rolling-element bearing
  anomaly detectors, and uses it to test whether SCADA-rate bin-averaging
  destroys detection lead time. Five datasets are used: four inferential
  run-to-failure campaigns - XJTU-SY (n=10), FEMTO/PRONOSTIA (n=6), University
  of Ferrara (n=6) and NASA IMS (n=3) - together with a single-asset
  real-historian case study on an ONGC gas-turbine SCADA record (n=1,
  descriptive only; no inferential claim depends on it). With run-level
  statistics and Holm correction, the headline finding is non-destruction:
  coarse SCADA-rate logging does not cost bearing-fault warning time. The
  archive ships the full harness, the derived result tables under
  results/tables/, and the derived ONGC artifacts; the ONGC raw waveforms,
  channel-level measurements and asset identifiers are proprietary and are not
  included.
keywords:
  - predictive maintenance
  - prognostics and health management
  - lead time
  - anomaly detection
  - condition monitoring
  - rolling-element bearing
  - SCADA
  - conformal prediction
  - statistical process control
license: MIT
version: 1.3.0
date-released: "2026-09-24"
